onale mis-states the mechanism's live behavior: it claims `expected` is FALSE on the production box today (gate inert, becomes strict only on a future deploy), but the actual deployment surface sets REBAR_TRACKER_DIR unconditionally, so per the plan's own `expected` definition the gate is STRICT on the first deploy of this change.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"facadf0b4d4be862f","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Plan section: 'How expected is computed' — final paragraph ('the production box today sets no tracker dir, so expected is false, so the gate is INERT')","norm_id":"n51191ee631e093bb","priority":0.9,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["First deploy of this change to the live box: the new blue/green container reports expected:true, so the gate is immediately strict rather than inert as the plan asserts.","If the tickets-branch clone/volume convergence (Dockerfile.mcp entrypoint) has not completed by the time the MCP_HEALTH_TIMEOUT readiness loop probes /health, `present` is false while `expected` is true, and the gate refuses the cutover the plan believes is a no-op."],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Replace the 'gate is inert today, becomes strict on a future deploy' claim with the accurate one (gate is active immediately), and add reasoning for the transient present:false while expected:true window during the Dockerfile.mcp first-boot clone: either the readiness loop's MCP_HEALTH_TIMEOUT (120s) is argued to always exceed convergence, or the boot sweep converges the store before /health can report present:false, so the initial cutover is not falsely refused.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.9,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes